{"parentUuid":"870fb9a8-f960-42b8-9e00-4706c48246dc","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aeef0d5e64a3cfeb9","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01CJMvrAY9YzPuCq78u5VtSz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01H9yGz7y56GzdbULYfWJPaS","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"or-tools\" \"drat_writer\" pure SAT only DRAT proof limitations"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3782,"cache_read_input_tokens":35866,"output_tokens":396,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3782},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":396,"cache_read_input_tokens":35866,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3782,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3782,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorkdVKAvMe9bELxCCrk","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3dd8f90d-3d0f-4f59-aae6-17509a37a793","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:56.015Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e2bf63af-3165-4236-ae7e-b8a8bca7126a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"aeef0d5e64a3cfeb9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QDxVLJy8bhP9PGh8MhdaqP","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary of GitHub Issue #973\n\nBased on the provided webpage content, I can only see the issue **title and description**, not any discussion or responses from Laurent Perron or other contributors.\n\n## What the Issue Contains\n\nThe original issue (opened December 11, 2018) asks whether CP-SAT has a built-in method to identify unsolvable constraints. The author proposes two potential approaches:\n\n1. Iteratively solving while excluding one element at a time\n2. Making constraints optional by introducing boolean variables and maximizing their satisfaction\n\n## What's Missing\n\nThe webpage excerpt does not include:\n- Laurent Perron's response or recommendations\n- Any discussion of extracting infeasible constraints\n- Information about workarounds for recovering learned clauses or UNSAT cores\n- Subsequent comments from other developers\n\nTo answer your specific questions about Perron's guidance, I would need access to the actual discussion/comments section of the issue, which isn't present in the provided content."}]},"uuid":"06e490df-4798-4fc8-bb43-e086f2ea28df","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:58.410Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e2bf63af-3165-4236-ae7e-b8a8bca7126a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fcaad7ed-ba0f-4e50-a9c8-116a114aff9f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"aeef0d5e64a3cfeb9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjyQxtgyU5WwUaMFQMCh3S","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"cp_model\" \"AddAssumption\" \"sufficient_assumptions\" python example minimal\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Assumptions\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/qlVv2uSq1uo\"},{\"title\":\"python cp-sat unsat assumption bug, is sat · Issue #2649 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2649\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"Solution hint and Assumptions reports wrong status when solved after presolve · Issue #4324 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4324\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT get's stuck when trying to maximize sum of boolean variables that are all added as assumptions · google/or-tools · Discussion #3630\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/discussions/3630\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"CPMpy ortools interface (cpmpy.solvers.ortools) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/pumpkin/api/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Modeling and solving with CPMpy — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/modeling.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about `cp_model`, `AddAssumption`, and `sufficient_assumptions` in Python:\n\n## Key Information\n\nThe `add_assumption` method adds a single literal to the model as assumptions, while `add_assumptions` adds multiple literals to the model as assumptions.\n\n## Minimal Example\n\nHere's a minimal example from the search results:\n\nYou can create boolean variables and add constraints with `OnlyEnforceIf`, then add them as assumptions using `model.AddAssumptions([i1,i2,i3,i4])`.\n\nBy using assumptions in conjunction with `sufficient_assumptions_for_infeasibility`, CP-SAT can automatically identify which constraints are responsible for infeasibility.\n\n## Important Limitations\n\nObjective and assumptions are incompatible - this is a critical constraint to be aware of when using assumptions.\n\nThe result from `SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility` is reduced by heuristics and not necessarily minimal, and currently will minimize the set only in a single thread and without an objective.\n\nA practical example showing how to use assumptions is available in the search results, where boolean variables are created, constraints are added with `OnlyEnforceIf()`, and then these assumptions are added to the model before solving.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"6891dc41-0df1-4aeb-9bfb-84d1e38c3104","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:01.162Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fcaad7ed-ba0f-4e50-a9c8-116a114aff9f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3dd8f90d-3d0f-4f59-aae6-17509a37a793","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"aeef0d5e64a3cfeb9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H9yGz7y56GzdbULYfWJPaS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"or-tools\" \"drat_writer\" pure SAT only DRAT proof limitations\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"DRAT Proofs of Unsatisfiability for SAT Modulo Monotonic Theories\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2401.10703v1\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/tree/stable/ortools/sat\"},{\"title\":\"DratProofHandler | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/reference/sat/drat_proof_handler/DratProofHandler\"},{\"title\":\"Efficient Proof Checking with LRAT in CADICAL (Work in Progress)\",\"url\":\"https://cca.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/PollittFleuryBiere-MBMV23.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - marijnheule/drat-trim: The DRAT-trim proof checker\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/marijnheule/drat-trim\"},{\"title\":\"DRAT Proofs of Unsatisfiability for SAT Modulo Monotonic Theories\",\"url\":\"https://assets.amazon.science/45/c8/bc0875b84cb3b5119cfbacc2e216/drat-proofs-of-unsatisfiability-for-sat-modulo-monotonic-theories.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Flexible Proof Format for SAT Solver-Elaborator Communication\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~mheule/publications/FRAT-TACAS.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"DRAT Proofs of Unsatisfiability for SAT Modulo Monotonic Theories | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-57246-3_1\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_solver.h at master · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/master/ortools/sat/sat_solver.h\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/drat_checker.h at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/drat_checker.h\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the OR-Tools `drat_writer` and pure SAT DRAT proof limitations:\n\n## OR-Tools DRAT Writer\n\nThe `drat_writer.h` in OR-Tools is used to write UNSAT proofs in the DRAT format. This allows checking the correctness of an UNSAT proof with the third-party program DRAT-trim.\n\n## Pure SAT Limitation\n\nThe OR-Tools SatSolver contains a boolean field `problem_is_pure_sat_` that indicates whether the loaded problem only contains clauses. This suggests that the DRAT proof generation in OR-Tools is designed for **pure SAT problems only** (those containing only clausal constraints).\n\n## DRAT Proof Limitations\n\nDRAT proofs are often huge (can be GB), and take about as much time to check as it takes for the solver to find the proof in the first place! \n\nThe proof format DRAT used in the SAT competition is rather inefficient to check, often even slower to check than it takes the SAT solver to solve the instance and generate the proof.\n\n## Beyond Pure SAT\n\nFor problems that extend beyond pure SAT (like SMT problems with theories), the research shows that when SMT solvers use DRAT-based approaches, the final proof certificate is not purely DRAT, and any theory lemmas must be checked by theory-specific certificate checkers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"b1953c53-fced-45b7-8686-17b509045394","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:02.697Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3dd8f90d-3d0f-4f59-aae6-17509a37a793","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"870fb9a8-f960-42b8-9e00-4706c48246dc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"aeef0d5e64a3cfeb9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126btWpcBmioedY8Gfb8F4X","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Benders decomposition CP-SAT nogood cut lazy constraint LBBD callback\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition - John Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/encyLBBD.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/logic-based-benders-decomposition-lbbd-4e47b3ae-690a-44a4-9d80-a191b385b4e6\"},{\"title\":\"Solving logic-based benders decomposition master problems with constraint programming and domain-independent dynamic programming | Constraints | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10601-025-09382-1\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Evaluation of Cut-Strengthening Techniques in Logic-Based Benders’ Decomposition | Operations Research Forum | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43069-023-00242-3\"},{\"title\":\"Combining Optimisation and Simulation Using Logic Based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.08390\"},{\"title\":\"Integrating Benders Decomposition Within Constraint Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/11564751_58\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Benders decomposition in Constraint Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/230669092_Benders_decomposition_in_Constraint_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"Combining optimisation and simulation using logic-based Benders decomposition - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S037722172300591X\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders Decomposition for Large-scale Optimization J. N. Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/LBBDbookChapter.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders Decomposition and Binary Decision Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/7320.pdf\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for your query about Benders decomposition, CP-SAT, nogood cuts, lazy constraints, LBBD, and callbacks. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Logic-Based Benders Decomposition (LBBD) Overview\n\nLBBD is a generalization of classical Benders decomposition that enables superior integration of combinatorial, constraint-oriented, and non-convex subproblem structures, allowing the master problem and subproblems to be solved using heterogeneous optimization technologies with logic-based inference used for generating valid cuts.\n\n## Nogood Cuts\n\nNogood or exclusion cuts encode that the current master assignment leads to infeasibility in the subproblem, with a minimal infeasible subsystem (IIS) or concrete \"nogood\" pattern extracted and used to cut off that assignment. No-good cuts originate from work on unit hypercubes and are the tightest inequalities which separate a single corner of the hypercube.\n\n## Lazy Constraints and Callbacks\n\nRather than rebuilding a Branch-and-Bound tree from scratch in each iteration, in practice, Benders cuts are added as lazy constraints during a callback routine. Implementing LBBD effectively requires careful design of cut management through lazy callbacks and cut pool truncation, often tailored to commercial solvers such as Gurobi, CPLEX, IBM CP Optimizer.\n\n## Cut-Strengthening Techniques\n\nCut-strengthening techniques have significant impact on the computational effectiveness of LBBD, with techniques that generate irreducible cuts outperforming greedy algorithms and the use of no cut strengthening.\n\nThe search results provide comprehensive information about combining these techniques for solving complex optimization problems with heterogeneous solver components.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4c4fe595-9c3e-4b95-9553-001e3f8e09ed","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:03.377Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"870fb9a8-f960-42b8-9e00-4706c48246dc","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
